      'Which type of stucco is best? The answer depends on your climate, your budget, and the look you want. There are four main stucco systems used in residential and commercial construction, and each has distinct strengths and weaknesses. Here is what you need to know to make the right choice — especially if you are building or renovating in San Antonio.',
      '## Traditional Three-Coat Cement Stucco',
      'Three-coat stucco is the gold standard. It consists of a scratch coat, brown coat, and finish coat applied over metal lath, totaling about seven-eighths of an inch thick. It is fire-resistant, breathable, impact-resistant, and lasts 50 to 80 years when properly installed. For San Antonio homes, three-coat cement stucco is the best all-around choice because it handles our extreme heat, humidity, and UV exposure better than any alternative. The downside is that it is rigid and will crack with foundation movement, but cracks are easy to repair.',
      '## One-Coat Stucco',
      'One-coat stucco is a fiber-reinforced Portland cement product applied in a single layer, typically three-eighths of an inch thick. It was developed to save labor time and became common in production homebuilding during the 1990s and 2000s. While it performs adequately in mild climates, one-coat stucco is thinner and more prone to cracking and moisture intrusion than a full three-coat system. In San Antonio, where thermal cycling and soil movement are constant, one-coat systems tend to develop problems 15 to 25 years in. If you are building new, three-coat is worth the extra cost.',
      '## EIFS (Synthetic Stucco)',
      'EIFS uses foam insulation board covered with a thin acrylic base coat, fiberglass mesh, and a synthetic finish. It provides excellent insulation and design flexibility — you can create detailed architectural features that would be impractical in cement stucco. The trade-off is that EIFS is soft, easily damaged by impact, and depends entirely on sealed joints to keep water out. When water gets behind EIFS it has nowhere to go, which can cause hidden rot and mold. EIFS is best for custom homes where energy efficiency and design detail are priorities, and where the homeowner will maintain sealant joints rigorously.',
      '## Acrylic Finish Over Cement Base',
      'Some systems combine a cement scratch and brown coat with an acrylic polymer finish coat instead of a traditional cement finish. This gives you the structural strength of cement stucco with the color consistency and flexibility of an acrylic topcoat. Acrylic finishes resist cracking better than pure cement finishes and offer a wider range of colors. The downside is that acrylic finishes are less breathable than cement and can trap moisture if not applied over a properly cured base coat.',
      '## Which Type Is Best for San Antonio?',
      'For most San Antonio homes, traditional three-coat cement stucco is the best choice. It is the most durable, the most forgiving, and the best suited to our demanding climate. EIFS makes sense for specific custom projects where insulation and design are top priorities. One-coat should generally be avoided for new construction in South Texas. If you are not sure which system is on your home or which to choose for a new project, our <a href="/stucco-installation">stucco installation team</a> can evaluate your situation and recommend the right system for your property.',
